subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $k$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 3\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+k\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the first components, we get\n\n$$\n3k=-6\\,⟹\\,k=-2.\n$$\n\nBut then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is no $k$ such that $3{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186542367 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186119805.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186542367 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "654ebed8-c886-576a-ae1a-ac9f64553608" #edb/ref 17592186045443]}
